ferrometer (0.5.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * New `hwmon` sub-collector (unix collector, on by default) exporting
    /sys/class/hwmon sensors under node_exporter's names:
    node_hwmon_temp_celsius, node_hwmon_fan_rpm, node_hwmon_in_volts,
    node_hwmon_curr_amps, node_hwmon_power_watt, node_hwmon_humidity,
    node_hwmon_energy_joule_total, their _min_/_max_/_crit_ threshold
    variants, the _alarm/_crit_alarm flags, node_hwmon_sensor_label and
    node_hwmon_chip_names. Series carry a `disk` label when the chip belongs
    to exactly one block device, so a drive temperature joins with
    node_disk_* and node_md_disk_*. NVMe works out of the box; SATA/SAS
    needs the drivetemp module loaded. No daemon and no privileges: this
    replaces what the unmaintained hddtemp service was for. New
    `hwmon-chips` include-regex narrows collection (a many-core coretemp
    chip is over a hundred series on its own).
  * New `md` sub-collector (unix collector, on by default) reporting Linux
    software-RAID state from /sys/block/md*/md: node_md_degraded,
    node_md_disks{state}, node_md_disks_required, node_md_state{state},
    node_md_blocks, node_md_blocks_synced, node_md_sync_speed_bytes,
    node_md_mismatch_cnt, node_md_chunk_size_bytes, node_md_info,
    node_md_last_sync_action, plus a per-member family (node_md_disk_state,
    node_md_disk_errors_total, node_md_disk_bad_blocks,
    node_md_disk_unacknowledged_bad_blocks, node_md_disk_size_bytes,
    node_md_disk_slot). Names match node_exporter's mdadm collector where it
    has an equivalent. No privileges and no mdadm subprocess; hosts with no
    md array pay one stat per tick.

 -- Roman Kivalin <roman@shl.dev>  Sat, 29 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

ferrometer (0.4.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Prometheus scraper emits `up` (1/0), `scrape_duration_seconds` and
    `scrape_samples_scraped` every tick (static labels + scraper="<name>"),
    so a down target shows as up==0 instead of a missing series. Scrape
    failures are logged on transitions only (warn on down, info on
    recovery) instead of once per tick.
  * journald source: new `batch-max-bytes` option (default "1M" = 1,000,000
    bytes; SI units, `MiB` etc. for binary) caps each batch by approximate
    encoded size in addition to the `batch-size` entry count. Applies to
    fresh batches and to the top-up of an in-flight batch during backoff.
  * Fix: journald -> Loki shipping could wedge permanently after a sink
    outage. The backlog was batched by entry count only, so a batch of large
    entries could exceed Loki's 4 MiB gRPC message limit (ResourceExhausted)
    and be retried verbatim forever. Batches are now also byte-capped.
  * Fix: the log shipper recovers from payload-too-large rejections (HTTP 413
    or a body reporting a message-size limit): it re-sends the batch in
    progressively smaller chunks instead of retrying verbatim, and drops —
    with an error log — any single entry the sink refuses on its own.
  * Fix: Loki HTTP 400 validation rejections (timestamp too old, line too
    long, bad labels, ...) are no longer retried forever. Loki ingests the
    valid entries and drops the offending ones, so the shipper logs the
    rejection at error level and acks the batch.
  * Loki error bodies are trimmed before logging (no more two-line warnings).

 -- Roman Kivalin <roman@shl.dev>  Wed, 19 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

ferrometer (0.3.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Filesystem collector dedupes bind mounts: by default only one entry per
    block device is reported (root="/" wins, ties broken by mountpoint). New
    `filesystem-mount-points` / `filesystem-fs-types` include-regex options
    narrow further; `filesystem-dedupe-devices = false` restores previous
    behavior. Switches from /proc/mounts to /proc/self/mountinfo.
  * HTTP clients now validate server certificates against the OS trust store
    instead of a baked-in Mozilla CA bundle. Admin-installed CAs in
    /etc/ssl/certs are picked up automatically; `ca-cert-file` still adds
    a private CA on top.

 -- Roman Kivalin <roman@shl.dev>  Wed, 13 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000

ferrometer (0.2.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Logs auto-format for journald when stderr is hooked to it: timestamps
    dropped (journald has its own), ANSI colors dropped, syslog `<N>`
    priority prefix per line so journald assigns the right PRIORITY field.
    Otherwise the human-friendly format is unchanged.
  * Unix collector emits `node_memory_Dirty_bytes`.

 -- Roman Kivalin <roman@shl.dev>  Sun, 26 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000

ferrometer (0.1.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Initial release.
  * Collectors: Unix system metrics (CPU + frequency/governor/throttle,
    memory, disk incl. discards/flushes, filesystem, network, load average,
    uname); Prometheus scraper for any text-exposition /metrics endpoint.
  * Log shipping: systemd journal source with persisted cursor, Loki sink
    speaking the native protobuf + snappy push API. Per-shipper exponential
    backoff. journald itself is the durable buffer during a sink outage.
  * Forwarders: OTLP HTTP push with gzip compression, in-memory ring buffer,
    exponential backoff, configurable OTLP Resource attributes.
  * Auth: basic / bearer / arbitrary Authorization header on every HTTP
    client. Secrets via env vars, files, or systemd encrypted credentials.
  * mTLS with optional custom CA bundle on every HTTP client.
  * TOML configuration with ${instance.name} / ${version} / ${env:VAR}
    placeholder expansion and load-time validation.
  * Hardened systemd service unit running under a transient DynamicUser
    inside a locked-down sandbox.

 -- Roman Kivalin <roman@shl.dev>  Sat, 25 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000
